;; See docs/devtour/README.md for the format and how to add a block.
;;
;; Each step anchors on {:ns <namespace> :defn <symbol>} — NEVER a line number.
;; Variants: {:file "resources/packages/.../impls.clj" :defn <sym>} for package
;; impls outside src/, and {:defn ig/init-key :dispatch <val>} for a defmethod.
;; The generator bakes the anchored form's real source into index.html; CI
;; (`bb devtour-check`) fails if an anchor stops resolving or the HTML drifts.
{:title "Graphden — developer tour"
 :intro
 "Welcome. This is a guided read of the graphden codebase for someone who
  just joined and needs to find their feet in the whole system — not the
  user tutorial (that lives in `docs/tutorial/` and is about *using* the
  editor), but the host code that makes it run.

  The system splits into a handful of relatively independent **blocks**
  (left). Read them roughly top-to-bottom: each block's `after:` line names
  what it assumes you have already seen. Start with the **Executor** — it is
  the spine everything else hangs off — then follow the dependency order:
  Storage, then Branches, Types, CRUD, Packages, Boot, Web, Layout, the
  Editor frontend, Services, Platform seams, Accounts. (The Platform block
  tours the in-repo seams and hardening; the multi-tenant / cloud POLICY
  layer itself is in the private graphden-tenancy repo — the open-core
  split — plugging into the `graphden.tenancy.context` seam kept here.)

  Two blocks are worth knowing about before you need them. **Boot** answers
  'what actually happens when the process starts', which is the fastest way
  to see how the other blocks are wired together — read it early if you are
  more comfortable starting from a running system than from a hot path. The
  **Editor frontend** block tours JavaScript rather than Clojure; it is baked
  and drift-checked exactly like the rest.

  Every step shows you the **actual source** of one function, pulled straight
  from the file it lives in. When a step points you at code in another block,
  use the *see also* chips — **Back** always returns you along the path you
  actually walked.

  This tour is about the code, not the workflow. For how to BUILD and test it
  — `bb ci`, the linters, the isolated worktrees and the serialized merge
  queue — read [CLAUDE.md](../../CLAUDE.md) and
  [dev/wtq/AGENT.md](../../dev/wtq/AGENT.md)."

 :blocks
 [{:id :executor
   :title "Executor — how a fn runs"
   :status :toured
   :summary "The spine: turn a graph 'description of a computation' into a value."
   :paths ["src/graphden/executor/"]
   :after []
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.executor.interface :defn execute
     :say "**Start here.** `execute` is the public front door of the whole
           system. A caller hands it an execution *context*, a `fn-id` — the id
           of a row in the graph that *describes* a computation — and a map of
           free args. This ns is a thin, validating façade: it checks the args
           shape and delegates to the real machinery in `compile-runtime`.
           Everything below is how that graph *description* becomes a value."
     :see [[:executor "create-context"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile-runtime :defn execute
     :say "The dispatch heart of the hot path. It checks the cancellation /
           effect / execute-guard gates, pulls the **compiled registry** out of
           the context, looks up the closure for this `fn-id` (or throws
           fn-not-found), translates the caller's name-keyed args into the dual
           slot-id+name shape the compiled code expects, and finally calls
           `(closure translated ctx)`. This is the moment *execute a fn* becomes
           *call a precompiled closure*."
     :see [[:executor "registry"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.context :defn create-context
     :say "Builds the `ExecutionContext` the previous step reads from. It
           carries the storage handle, the `:base-fns` impl registry
           (snapshotted from the global default), a lazily-filled
           `:compiled-registry` holder, the graph/deps caches, and the
           authz/effect seams. Because scoping (e.g. which branch) lives here,
           the *same* `fn-id` run in two contexts can resolve to different
           code — this is where that happens."}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile-runtime :defn registry
     :say "Returns the `{fn-id -> closure}` map from the context holder. A warm
           holder is served straight back; if a full clear flagged it stale it
           is still served immediately while a background `rebuild-optimistic!`
           refreshes it (stale-while-revalidate — no reader ever blocks behind a
           ~50s cold compile). Only a genuinely COLD holder (boot, a divergent
           cold branch) compiles on demand under the invalidation lock, double-
           checked so concurrent callers coalesce onto one rebuild. This is the
           lazy **compile-once, reuse-forever** registry the executor runs against."
     :see [[:executor "rebuild!"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile-runtime :defn rebuild!
     :say "The compile-at-startup pipeline in one form: `read-graph` from
           storage, then `build-lookups`, then `compile-all`; finally it primes
           the graph/deps caches and resets the registry holder. This is the
           seam where **code = graph in DB** turns into runnable Clojure
           closures. The next few steps open up each stage it calls."
     :see [[:executor "build-lookups"] [:executor "compile-all"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.composition.core :defn sync-fns-to-storage!
     :say "The opposite direction — and the reason there is a graph to read at
           all. Authored fn-defs (from packages) are reconciled into fn / slot /
           binding rows in storage here. So the graph that `rebuild!` reads was
           itself *written* by this composition layer from source fn-defs. Read
           it to see how declarations become persisted graph entities."}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile.lookups :defn build-lookups
     :say "Indexes the raw graph rows — fns, slots, fn-slots, bindings,
           list-items — into the O(1) maps every compile helper consumes
           (`:fn-map`, `:slot-map`, `:bindings-by-fn`, the inheritance-chain
           cache, …). This is the in-memory materialization of the DB graph that
           the whole compile pass walks over."}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile.lookups :defn root-fn
     :say "Walks a composed fn's `parent-ids` inheritance chain up to the
           parentless **root** that owns the slots and carries a registered
           impl. This is the base-fn resolution step: how an arbitrary composed
           `fn-id` maps to the primitive whose Clojure impl will actually run."}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile.bindings :defn collect-bindings
     :say "Classifies each of a fn's root slots (via `classify-slot`) into a
           `:value` / `:free` / `:ref` / `:seq` / `:secret-value` entry,
           memoised on the lookups. This classification is the pivot the code
           generator switches on next — the *shape* of every argument is decided
           here, once, at compile time rather than per execution."
     :see [[:executor "arg-builder"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile-eager :defn compile-all
     :say "Topologically orders every impl-backed fn and compiles each in
           dependency order into `{fn-id -> (fn [fa ctx])}`, cached process-wide
           by (graph-shape × base-fn set × ambient rich-types) — the rich-types
           component is load-bearing: `produces-callable?` HOF-wrap decisions
           depend on the swept `:return-type` entries, so callers under
           different types must not share a compile. Dependency order matters:
           by the time a parent compiles, its children's callables already
           exist to be wired in."
     :see [[:executor "compile-fn"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile-eager :defn compile-fn
     :say "Produces one fn's runtime closure `(fn [fa ctx])`. It turns each
           classified binding into a *builder*, wires env-bindings and
           rename-aliases, and at call time assembles the args map and invokes
           `(impl args ctx)`. This is the concrete anatomy of a single compiled
           graph node."
     :see [[:executor "arg-builder"] [:executor "resolve-impl"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile-eager :defn arg-builder
     :say "Emits the per-binding `(fn [fa ctx])`: `:value` becomes a constant,
           `:free` reads the slot-id (with a name fallback), `:ref` becomes a
           `thunk`/delay that **defers** the child call, `:seq` becomes a lazy
           seq. This is the origin of graphden's laziness — refs are *wrapped,
           not evaluated*, so native Clojure `if`/`and`/`or` short-circuit for
           free without the executor knowing their names."
     :see [[:executor "resolve-arg"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile-eager :defn resolve-impl
     :say "Looks the base-fn's Clojure impl out of `lookups[:base-fns]` by the
           root fn's name, throwing `:compile/missing-impl` on a gap. This is the
           concrete join between a graph node and the actual code that computes
           it — the point where the graph description meets a real function."
     :see [[:executor "defbase"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.defbase :defn defbase
     :say "The macro base-fn authors use. It rewrites a body that names its args
           as bare symbols into `(defn name [__args ctx] …)`, where each symbol
           reads through `resolve-arg`. This explains why the impls that
           `resolve-impl` finds look like ordinary Clojure even though their
           arguments arrive as a compiled map."
     :see [[:executor "resolve-arg"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.runtime :defn resolve-arg
     :say "The read-side of laziness. Inside an impl this forces a `thunk`
           (calling it once), derefs an `IDeref`/`delay`, or returns a plain
           value untouched. This is where a deferred `:ref` argument actually
           gets evaluated — at the moment, and only if, the impl reads it. Pair
           it with `arg-builder`, which created the thunk."
     :see [[:executor "arg-builder"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor.compile-eager :defn call-with-cache
     :say "Closes the loop. Child-callable invocations are memoised for the
           duration of one top-level `execute`, keyed by `[ref-id
           projected-free-args]` in a per-call map (with a `::nil` sentinel and a
           size cap). This is how the lazy graph avoids recomputing the same
           sub-result and delivers a single cached value back up to the caller —
           the DRY principle, enforced at runtime."}]}

  {:id :storage
   :title "Storage & the graph"
   :status :toured
   :summary "How the graph is stored and traversed — Postgres, recursive CTEs,
             the StorageCRUD / ExecutionGraph protocols."
   :paths ["src/graphden/storage/" "src/graphden/schema/"]
   :after [:executor]
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.storage.protocol.core :defn Storage
     :say "The storage layer starts with three protocols; this is the first.
           `Storage` is the lifecycle contract (`initialize` / `close`) every
           backend implements, binding a concrete store to a `DataSchema`
           before any graph can be read or written."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.protocol.core :defn StorageCRUD
     :say "The row-level contract: `read-entity`, `query-entities`,
           `create-entity`, and friends. Every graph read the executor does
           bottoms out in these calls — learn this shape and you know how the
           whole system touches the database."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.protocol.core :defn ExecutionGraph
     :say "One method: `resolve-execution-graph` — given a root fn-id, return
           its entire reachable subgraph in a single shot. This is the seam the
           executor's `rebuild!` reads through, and it is implemented twice
           below: a backend-agnostic version and a Postgres-optimized one."
     :see [[:executor "rebuild!"]]}
    {:ns graphden.schema.protocol.protocol :defn DataSchema
     :say "What a schema exposes: the entities, their fields, and the field
           types (`:ref`, `:enum`, `:union`). Rows are shaped by this vocabulary
           before storage ever sees them — so read it before the row-storage
           code below."}
    {:ns graphden.schema.malli.core :defn MalliDataSchema
     :say "…and its ONE implementation. The record is an immutable bag of maps
           (`entities-map`, `enums-map`, `entity-uuids-map`,
           `retired-fields-map`) with every protocol method a lookup into
           them — which is the point: a schema is data a builder assembled,
           not code. `create-builder` at the bottom of the file is what the
           `:db/schema` component calls, and the `extend-builder` fns below
           are how each subsystem contributes its entities to it."
     :see [[:boot "postgres"]]}
    {:ns graphden.schema.graph.schema :defn extend-builder
     :say "Where the graph model is actually declared: the `ns` / `fn` / `slot`
           / `binding` / `binding-list-item` entities and the `:ref` edges
           between them. This is the concrete table + foreign-key layout that
           every traversal walks."}
    {:ns graphden.schema.fields.types :defn valid-type?
     :say "The runtime link between a field-spec's declared type and an
           actual value: one predicate per abstract type, permissive on
           unknown types. `storage/protocol/crud_validation` runs this on
           every write — the live value-validation path (the old parallel
           malli validate-entity pipeline was dead code and is gone)."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.protocol.graph :defn ExecutionGraphResult
     :say "The record every resolver returns: the entity collections plus
           by-fn / by-binding indexes. Know this shape first and the two
           resolvers below read easily — they all just fill it in."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.protocol.graph :defn ->execution-graph
     :say "Builds that record and its O(1) convenience indexes — the shared
           `assemble the result` step both backends funnel into."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.protocol.graph :defn process-fn-node
     :say "Expands one fn during traversal, extracting its outgoing edges:
           parent-ids, base / element / return-type, and binding + list-item
           `ref-fn-id`s. This function IS the authoritative definition of what
           counts as a graph edge."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.protocol.graph :defn resolve-execution-graph-bfs
     :say "The backend-agnostic BFS driver: keep calling `process-fn-node`
           until the reachable closure is exhausted. This is the reference
           algorithm the Postgres recursive CTE mirrors."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.protocol.generic-graph :defn resolve-execution-graph
     :say "The default `ExecutionGraph` implementation — it wires the
           `StorageCRUD` loaders into the BFS. Correct on any backend, but it
           costs ~4N round-trips, which is exactly what motivates the Postgres
           specialization next."
     :see [[:storage "StorageCRUD"]]}
    {:ns graphden.storage.postgres.core :defn PostgresStorage
     :say "The concrete Postgres backend. Its `ExecutionGraph` extension
           delegates to the Postgres resolver instead of the generic BFS —
           showing how a backend overrides the default path when it can do
           better."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.postgres.migration :defn do-initialize
     :say "Nobody hand-writes DDL here: the schema of the previous steps is
           DATA, and this diffs it against the metadata table to emit exactly
           the changes needed — first-init or migration, all in one
           transaction. Read the docstring for the fleet property that makes
           it safe: a transaction-scoped advisory lock taken BEFORE the state
           read, so N pods booting against one database serialize, and the
           second one computes a no-op instead of re-planning against
           pre-DDL state. This is why a graphden upgrade needs no DB reset
           (docs/OPERATIONS.md)."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.postgres.graph :defn reachable-fns-sql
     :say "The `WITH RECURSIVE` CTE that walks every outgoing edge from the
           seed fn in one statement and returns the reachable `fn.id` set. This
           is the heart of the recursive-CTE traversal, mirroring in SQL exactly
           the edge list that `process-fn-node` computes in Clojure."
     :see [[:storage "process-fn-node"]]}
    {:ns graphden.storage.postgres.graph :defn resolve-execution-graph
     :say "The optimized resolver: run the CTE to get reachable ids, enforce
           the graph-size cap, bulk-load fn / slot / binding rows with five
           `IN`-clause queries, and hand off to `->execution-graph`. O(1)
           round-trips versus the generic O(N) — the payoff of the whole
           protocol split."
     :see [[:storage "->execution-graph"]]}]}

  {:id :versioning
   :title "Branches & versioning"
   :status :toured
   :summary "The VersionedStorage decorator, per-branch resolution, diff/merge
             and branch-local? propagation."
   :paths ["src/graphden/versioning/" "src/graphden/system/branch_router.clj"]
   :after [:storage]
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.versioning.storage.core :defn wrap-with-versioning
     :say "The entry point: take any base storage, ensure a `main` branch
           exists, and return a `VersionedStorage` bound to it. Start here to
           see how the entire versioning layer is bolted onto the plain storage
           from the previous block."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.core :defn VersionedStorage
     :say "The decorator record itself. It implements every storage protocol,
           delegating non-versioned entities straight to the base storage and
           intercepting versioned ones — reads resolve to a branch view, writes
           append version rows. Every method is a two-line dispatch: read this
           for the SHAPE, then follow the versioned arm into its own function.
           Grasp this structure before anything else here."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.core :defn create-entity-versioned!
     :say "The versioned arm of a single create, and the write path worth
           reading in full: identity row + version row, the identity-field diff
           for when the deterministic id already exists, and the advisory-lock
           branch that serialises the writes whose uniqueness is a per-branch
           RESOLVED-VIEW property (fn names, list-item positions) rather than a
           table constraint. `update-entity-versioned!` next to it tells the
           same story for the append case."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.resolution :defn entity-config
     :say "The authoritative map of WHICH entities are versioned (fn / fn-slot
           / binding / binding-list-item) and how each splits across two tables:
           `version-data-fields` live in the version table, identity fields stay
           on the base row. Everything downstream keys off this config."}
    {:ns graphden.schema.versioned.schema :defn derive-version-fields
     :say "Where the `-version` mirror entities come from: derived from the
           base entity's field map (refs demote to bare uuids, framework
           columns spliced in), never hand-copied. A new base column without a
           pinned mirror uuid or an identity-level declaration fails the build
           loudly — the old failure mode was the decorator silently dropping
           that column's writes. `version-data-fields` above derives from the
           same source, so the two can never disagree."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.resolution :defn resolve-version
     :say "The public per-branch read: for one entity, load its version chain,
           merge in a single batched pass, and return the winning version (nil
           on a tombstone). This is how a plain read becomes branch-aware."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.resolution :defn collect-branch-chain
     :say "The branch-chain walk: `[branch → parent → … → root]` via
           `:base-branch-id`, memoised process-wide. This inheritance backbone
           is what every resolve and cache-invalidation consults to know which
           branches a read can see."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.resolution :defn resolve-version-from-cache
     :say "The in-memory chain walk the resolver actually runs: at each branch
           level combine own-latest with merge candidates, pick the greatest
           `effective-ts`, and recurse to the parent. One tested algorithm shared
           by both single-entity and whole-graph reads."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.branch-local :defn effective-branch-local?
     :say "Monotonic-OR over the `:parent-ids` closure: a fn (or ANY ancestor)
           marked `:branch-local? true` is local forever. The resolver's merge
           filter calls this so runtime-config fns — web-server port, vault
           path, cron schedule — never leak across branches on merge."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.uniqueness :defn check-list-item-position-collisions!
     :say "The list-item position-collision guard, now in a shared
           `uniqueness` namespace so BOTH the write path (`core`) and the
           merge path (`merge`) enforce it without a require cycle. A
           cross-branch base index on `(binding-id, position)` would wrongly
           block divergence, so per-branch uniqueness is enforced here in the
           resolved view instead — a subtle invariant the write path defends
           with an advisory lock. Fn names follow the same doctrine:
           `check-fn-name-collision!` just below replaced the raw
           `UNIQUE (namespace_id, name)`, whose soft-deleted identities blocked
           their names forever."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.uniqueness :defn check-resource-override-path-collision!
     :say "The same resolved-view doctrine applied to the newest versioned
           entity: `:resource-override` (UI Step 1) shadows a shipped frontend
           asset — path → content, per branch — so the editor's own JS/CSS is
           editable from inside the editor (Operate → Assets). One override
           per path per branch view, guarded here; serving goes through
           `:read-resource-overridable` in the app package, and every save
           rolls the effective `?v=` asset hash so browsers re-fetch."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.core :defn purgeable-identity-ids
     :say "Delete has TWO modes, and this guard is the heart of the hard one.
           User-facing deletes tombstone (a version row with `:deleted-at`, so
           descendants hide the entity too); sync + rollback hard-delete. A
           hard delete used to drop only the branch's version rows — the
           surviving versionless identity became a GHOST that a later re-mint
           of the same deterministic id revived through the update path,
           content-equal, invisible on every list read (the 2026-07-20
           route-vanish incident). Now the identity row goes too, but ONLY when
           no other branch retains a version and no child identity still
           references it — per-branch isolation stays intact."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.identity-repair :defn repoint-refs!
     :say "The identity-plane repair primitives for the OTHER ghost source: a
           package fn's id is `uuid-v5(ns-path, name)`, so a namespace move
           mints a new id and abandons the old row with every ref still aimed
           at it. `repoint-refs!` fills refs in place across BOTH planes
           (identity and every branch's version rows); `purge-fn-subgraph!`
           below then removes the abandoned subgraph. The sync-time
           reconciler (`packages.sync/reconcile-moved-identities!`) runs them
           after every package sync, and the operator tool
           `graphden.dev.integrity` delegates to the same two."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.merge :defn diff-branches
     :say "The branch-symmetric resolved-view diff: gather every entity touched
           on either side's visibility set, resolve both, and report
           added/modified. The simplest read-only comparison — it precedes the
           conflict-oriented framing below."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.merge :defn detect-conflicts
     :say "Finds entities modified on BOTH branches after the computed fork
           point (skipping branch-local fns). This introduces fork-point
           reasoning and defines exactly what a merge must resolve before it can
           proceed."}
    {:ns graphden.versioning.storage.merge :defn merge-branch!
     :say "The merge itself: assert conflicts are resolved, then atomically
           write ONE `branch-merge` record — which makes the source's versions
           visible through resolution, copying no rows — plus any resolution
           overrides. The payoff step tying resolution and conflict detection
           together."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.postgres.graph-epoch :defn bump!
     :say "The graph EPOCH — freshness as a comparison, not an
           imperative. Every graph-shaped write bumps this sequence
           BEFORE writing; the router's fetch-time check
           (`validate-graph-epoch!`) heals any cached ctx whose eager
           invalidation was skipped — a client abort mid-request, a
           write path with no NOTIFY, a lost NOTIFY. The eager delta
           machinery and NOTIFY remain pure latency optimizations,
           which is what their docstrings always claimed."}
    {:ns graphden.system.branch-router :defn build-branch-ctx
     :say "Builds a fresh live `ExecutionContext` whose storage is a
           `VersionedStorage` rewrapped at the requested branch, with its own
           compiled-registry and graph-cache atoms. This is how a branch gets
           its own executor without re-sharding the graph."
     :see [[:executor "create-context"]]}
    {:ns graphden.system.branch-router :defn dispatch*
     :say "The top-level Ring dispatch: extract the branch ref (header or
           query), resolve it to a branch-id, and route the request to that
           branch's lazily-built handler (400 on an unknown branch). Where a
           live request finally lands in the correct per-branch context. Its
           public wrapper `dispatch` first short-circuits `/livez` — the one
           registry-independent liveness path — so a pod busy on a full
           recompile still answers its probe."}]}

  {:id :types
   :title "Type system"
   :status :toured
   :summary "Subtyping, unification, narrowing, refinements, the sweep-at-zero
             checker, and the :secret information-flow marker."
   :paths ["src/graphden/types/"]
   :after [:executor]
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.types.core.shapes :defn primitives
     :say "Start with the leaf vocabulary. This is the flat enum of primitive
           value-kinds plus the special `:any` (top) and `:never` (bottom). Every
           type in the system is built from these, so nothing structural makes
           sense until you have them."}
    {:ns graphden.types.core.shapes :defn child-types
     :say "The type grammar in one function: every compound arm — record maps,
           `[:list]`, `[:map]`, `[:tuple]`, `[:refine]`, `[:secret]`, `[:union]`,
           `[:fn]` — and how each recurses. This doubles as the map of how
           records / unions / variants / fn-types are represented."}
    {:ns graphden.types.core :defn well-formed?
     :say "The syntactic validity guard at the boundary between user input
           (fns.edn / the API) and the rest of the module. Reading it cements the
           exact shape of each type kind: fn args-map, effects set, refine base,
           secret inner."}
    {:ns graphden.types.core :defn make-union
     :say "The smart constructor for `[:union …]`: flatten, dedupe, absorb
           `:never`, and drop any member subsumed by another. This canonical
           union shape is what the rest of the system relies on — and your
           introduction to sum types."}
    {:ns graphden.types.core :defn desugar-variant
     :say "Rewrites `[:variant tag T …]` into a structural union of tag-pinned
           records. Shows how the higher-level variant sugar reduces to the union
           + refinement machinery you just met — no new runtime concept needed."}
    {:ns graphden.types.core :defn subtype?
     :say "The heart of assignability: reflexive / transitive subtyping across
           primitives, `:jsonb` sinks, records, lists, maps, tuples, refinements,
           unions, secrets, and fn-types. Every checker decision — is `actual`
           a subtype of `expected`? — bottoms out here."}
    {:ns graphden.types.core :defn constraint-implies?
     :say "Decides when one refinement constraint entails another (numeric
           bounds, `:in` / `:=` / `:not=`, `:and` / `:or`). This is exactly how
           `subtype?` compares refinement types like `:positive-int` ⊆
           `:non-negative-int`, so read it right after."
     :see [[:types "subtype?"]]}
    {:ns graphden.types.core :defn unify
     :say "Robinson unification with occurs-check and subtype-aware leniency,
           plus a union fallback. This is how polymorphic type variables get
           bound during checking — the two-way complement to the one-way
           `subtype?` test."}
    {:ns graphden.types.core :defn strip-null
     :say "The single source of truth for nullability narrowing: remove `:null`
           from a top-level union (bare `:null` → `:never`) while NEVER stripping
           a `:secret`. The canonical example of type narrowing, shared by
           control-flow refinement and the `:coalesce` rule."}
    {:ns graphden.types.core.shapes :defn taint-with-secret-if-tainted
     :say "The `:return-type-rule` propagator that wraps a fn's return in
           `[:secret …]` when any argument carries a secret anywhere. This is how
           the information-flow marker flows through composition — the payoff of
           the secret arm you saw in `subtype?`."
     :see [[:types "subtype?"]]}
    {:ns graphden.types.check.literals :defn classify-literal
     :say "Infers a structural type from a literal Clojure value (records,
           header maps, `[:list T]`, primitives). This is where concrete argument
           values enter the checker as types — the input side of every binding
           check."}
    {:ns graphden.types.check :defn check-binding!
     :say "Verifies one binding's `actual` ⊆ `expected`, running `unify` when
           type-vars are present and handling the `:any` / `:never` /
           refinement-on-literal escape hatches. The atomic unit of
           type-checking, tying together `subtype?`, `unify`, and
           `classify-literal`."}
    {:ns graphden.types.check :defn signature-return
     :say "The declared-signature engine: when a root base-fn has no hand
           `:return-type-rule`, the polymorphic declaration itself computes
           the return — unify each var-carrying declared arg against the
           actual type at the site, resolve the declared return through the
           substitution. `:coll [:list a] → [:union :null a]` IS the rule;
           the hand-written rules that survive are only those reading
           literal values, union fan-out, or the registry."}
    {:ns graphden.types.check :defn check-fn-def!
     :say "Type-checks a single fn-def against its (single or multiple-
           inheritance) parents: resolve parent slots, run the monotonicity /
           unknown-slot / effect guards, unify bindings, compute the return via
           type-rules, propagate effects, and snapshot the rich-type into the
           registry. The per-node checker the sweep repeats."}
    {:ns graphden.types.check :defn check-all-defs!
     :say "The whole-graph sweep entry point: fold `check-fn-def!` over
           topologically-ordered fn-defs so each def's computed rich-type is
           registered before its dependents read it. This is how the entire graph
           is checked end to end."}
    {:ns graphden.executor.registry.core :defn record-rich-types!
     :say "Where the sweep's answers are KEPT. The rich-types registry is the
           in-memory `{fn → {:return :args :effects …}}` snapshot every
           downstream consumer reads: the compiler's HOF-wrap decision, the
           executor's effect gate, secret redaction on persist, the editor's
           type chips. Aliases resolve to their structural body here, so
           everyone downstream sees one canonical shape."
     :see [[:executor "compile-all"] [:web "api-rich-types"]]}
    {:ns graphden.executor.registry.core :defn rich-type-of-id
     :say "…and how it is READ — by fn **id**, not name. Names are
           per-namespace labels and may repeat; ids cannot
           (docs/adr/ADR-identity-model.md, enforced in CI by
           `id_resolution_guard_test`). The `:by-name` index survives only for
           base-fns and legacy call sites. Before you key any new registry or
           dispatch by name, read the ADR — this is the mistake it exists to
           stop."}]}

  {:id :crud
   :title "CRUD & the write path"
   :status :toured
   :summary "Entity/branch/secret writes, request parsing, type-check on write,
             fn-execution lookup + persist."
   :paths ["src/graphden/crud/"]
   :after [:storage :types]
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.crud.request :defn extract-entity-params
     :say "The HTTP boundary where a mutation first becomes structured data:
           pull `type` / `id` out of a write request, preferring reitit
           `:path-params` and falling back to raw-URI parsing."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.request :defn read-json-body
     :say "Normalizes the Ring body (string / InputStream / already-parsed) into
           a keyword-keyed Clojure map — the payload every entity write is built
           from."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.branches :defn resolve-branch-ref
     :say "Resolves the target branch (by UUID or name) so a write lands on the
           right versioned storage. The branch dimension enters here, before any
           row is touched."
     :see [[:versioning "wrap-with-versioning"]]}
    {:ns graphden.crud.entities :defn create-entity
     :say "The generic single-row write entry point, and the best first stop for
           the whole write path: it sequences every stage — capability gate,
           `write-rej` validation, `sp/create-entity`, then invalidate + notify."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.validation :defn write-rej
     :say "The pre-write rejection gate that `create-entity` / `update-entity`
           consult. It centralizes the cycle / MI-collision / terminal /
           list-closed / secret-path checks that can veto a mutation before it
           ever persists."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.entities.record-type :defn apply-create-record-type-body
     :say "A multi-row atomic write unit: fn-row + N slot-rows + N fn-slot
           junctions, appending each success to a shared `journal` atom. Shows
           how one logical create fans out into many graph rows."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.entities.record-type :defn apply-create-rollback
     :say "The `:try` on-throw partner: replay the `journal` in reverse,
           best-effort deleting each row. This is how atomicity is achieved here
           WITHOUT a DB transaction — the compensating half of the body above."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.entities :defn apply-create-core
     :say "The atomic core for generic creates: create-or-humanise-error, a
           post-create whole-fn type-check, and the rename-slot side-effect.
           Since error-tolerance Phase 2 an ORDINARY type-check failure no
           longer rolls the row back — the write is kept and the diagnostics
           surface as `:type-warnings` on the success envelope. One class is
           carved out: a SECRET-flow diagnostic still deletes the row and
           returns the legacy `{:error …}` (the guarantee must not rest on
           the derived diagnostics store — docs/SECRETS.md)."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.type-check :defn type-check-fn-after-mutation!
     :say "Reconstructs the touched fn-def and runs `check-fn-def!` after a
           mutation, recording failures in (and clearing fixes from) the
           per-branch diagnostics store. This is the type-check-on-write that
           `apply-create-core` relies on — an ill-typed fn persists, but never
           silently: every save re-derives its diagnostics. The
           `:reject-secret?` option is the security carve-out: a
           secret-involving failure is flagged `:secret? true` and NOT
           recorded — the caller rolls the write back and hard-rejects."
     :see [[:types "check-fn-def!"]]}
    {:ns graphden.crud.entities.invalidation :defn notify-after-write!
     :say "The post-persist step: invalidate caches and broadcast a NOTIFY delta
           so sibling pods rebuild the affected fns. How a committed write becomes
           visible fleet-wide."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.fn-execution.lookup :defn resolve-fn
     :say "Translates the `/api/execute` `{:fn-id | :fn-name}` request into the
           full `:fn` row in one round-trip — the lookup that begins the
           execution + persist path."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.fn-execution :defn apply-execute
     :say "The `/api/execute` orchestrator: resolve fn + version + free-arg
           slots, acquire a concurrency slot, submit the future, and dispatch on
           succeeded / pending / failed. The spine of the execute write path —
           and where it hands off to the executor from the first block."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.fn-execution.persist :defn create-pending-with-args!
     :say "Atomically writes the `:fn-execution` row plus its per-arg
           (`:fn-execution-arg` / `-item`) rows — the concrete `an execution
           becomes persisted graph rows` step."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.fn-execution.persist :defn write-finished!
     :say "Updates the execution row with the future's terminal outcome (result
           / error / cancelled, with taint redaction). Closes the loop so a
           polled execution reflects final persisted state."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.value-repr :defn render-repr
     :say "Typed value representations (docs/EXECUTION.md § Typed value
           representations) — the execute-result pane's first dispatch tier
           and the value-form registry's read-side sibling: resolve a repr fn
           through the graph-held `:_value-repr-registry` (declared/inferred
           return type first, literal classification as fallback), execute it
           as a pure `value → hiccup` fn under the `:allowed-effects #{}`
           runtime gate, then push the output through the
           `web.hiccup-sanitize` allowlist before the editor inlines it into
           its own DOM."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.debug-capture :defn run-captured!
     :say "The «catch next request» debugger entry (docs/EXECUTION.md § Debug):
           a one-shot, TTL-bounded, org+branch-keyed trap consumed in
           `branch-router/ring-callable-for-ctx` runs the next matching HTTP
           request through this wrapper — Debug path-trace bound, sanitized
           request + response persisted as a standard `:fn-execution` row —
           so a live handler run replays through the same call-tree UI as a
           traced `/api/execute`."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.secrets :defn create-secret
     :say "The write path that must not leak (docs/SECRETS.md): one
           `POST /api/secrets` atomically creates the OpenBao value, its
           custom-metadata, and the graphden fn-row + path-binding — with a
           vault-delete rollback if the graph half fails. The secret VALUE
           never enters the graph; only the path does, on a `:secret-leaf`
           child the executor auto-derefs through the ctx's vault handle.
           Note the shape: this public defn is thin orchestration over the
           same per-stage helpers the `:cond` in `app/secrets/fns.edn`
           consumes, so the Clojure and the graph path can never diverge."
     :see [[:types "taint-with-secret-if-tainted"]]}
    {:ns graphden.crud.test-runs :defn run-tests!
     :say "The tests subsystem (docs/TESTS.md) rides everything above: a test
           is a fn in a `tests`-segment namespace, executed through
           `apply-execute` with `{}` args (pass = no throw), its status the
           newest execution of its CURRENT version — so an edited test reads
           `stale` by construction, no bookkeeping."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.test-autorun :defn schedule-affected!
     :say "Auto-run on writes: from `invalidate!`'s seeds, walk the
           compile-deps REVERSE closure (the service-restart blast walk),
           intersect with the branch's tests, gate on an EMPTY effect closure,
           and re-run debounced on a conveyed-bindings future with
           `:allowed-effects #{}` as the runtime backstop."}]}

  {:id :packages
   :title "Packages — the graph's content"
   :status :toured
   :summary "How fns.edn + impls.clj become base-fns and fn-defs; the loader,
             load order, and the first-party package tree — then the inverse:
             export, the ref wire form, and the publish/install registry."
   :paths ["src/graphden/packages/" "resources/packages/"]
   :after [:executor]
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn load-default-packages
     :say "The newcomer's entry point: it just calls `load-packages [core web
           app]`, naming the default package set and showing where the whole
           disk-to-graph pipeline is kicked off at boot."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn load-packages
     :say "The top-level orchestrator: resolve dependency order, load each
           package, and fold every module's results into the combined
           `{:base-fn-defs … :fn-defs … :namespaces …}` map that seeds the graph.
           The hub the rest of this block drills into."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn resolve-dependencies
     :say "A DFS topological sort over each `package.edn`'s `:dependencies`,
           pulling transitive deps so primitives load before their consumers.
           This is the dependency-driven load order."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn validate-dep-constraints!
     :say "Runs after ordering to check declared semver constraints against the
           classpath, failing fast at boot with `:packages/version-conflict`
           instead of a cryptic `Unknown parent` later. The version machinery
           guarding load order."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn load-single-package
     :say "For one package: read its `package.edn` metadata and reduce over its
           `:modules`, merging each module's base-fn-defs and fn-defs. The bridge
           from `a package` down to `its modules`."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn process-module
     :say "The heart of one module: load its `fns.edn` and `impls.clj`, then
           `group-by` splits the entries into base-fns (which need impls) versus
           fn-defs (compositions / type-rows). Where fns.edn and impls.clj first
           meet."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn base-fn?
     :say "The classifier `process-module` groups on: an entry is a base-fn only
           if it has no `:parent` / `:parents` and is not a type-row. This is the
           fundamental base-fn versus fn-def distinction a newcomer must
           internalize."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn load-module-fns
     :say "Reads a module's `fns.edn` (pure EDN data) via a source-position
           reader, attaching `:source-file` / `:source-line` to every fn-def so
           later type errors point at `file:line`. How the on-disk declarations
           enter memory."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn load-impls-via-eval
     :say "The counterpart to fns.edn: slurp `impls.clj`, `eval` each form in a
           fresh namespace, and return that namespace's `impls` var — the
           `{fn-name → impl-fn}` map. How Clojure code on disk becomes live
           functions."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn fn-def->base-fn-def
     :say "The glue: marry an fns.edn entry (args, return-type, effects, tags)
           with its impl-map value into the registry-shaped
           `{:args … :return-type … :impl fn …}`. Literally where fns.edn and
           impls.clj fuse into a base-fn."
     :see [[:executor "resolve-impl"]]}
    {:file "resources/packages/core/arithmetic/impls.clj" :defn add
     :say "A concrete `defbase` base-fn impl — `(defbase add [nums] (safe-arith
           + nums :add))`. This is the value `load-impls-via-eval` collects and
           `fn-def->base-fn-def` pairs with the `:add` entry in fns.edn. Grounds
           the abstract loader in a real arithmetic primitive."
     :see [[:executor "defbase"]]}
    {:ns graphden.packages.loader :defn sync-namespaces!
     :say "Closes the loop into storage: take the collected namespace paths,
           build the `core` → `core.arithmetic` parent-child `:ns` entity
           hierarchy, and return `{ns-path → id}` that the fn-def sync hangs the
           live fn rows under."
     :see [[:executor "sync-fns-to-storage!"]]}

    {:ns graphden.packages.sync :defn sync-fn-entities-from-packages!
     :say "The other half of every boot, and the one the loader hands off to:
           sync composed fn-defs to storage, heal namespace-moved identities,
           seed each fn-def's rich-type, then run the topological type-check
           sweep. Read the two long comments — the batch-size binding (a
           fresh-DB boot writes >10k fn rows past the guard meant for USER
           writes) and `:skip-type-check?` (~15s of sweep the test bootstrap
           may opt out of, production never does). This is declarative sync:
           the packages on disk are the desired state, storage is reconciled
           toward it."
     :see [[:types "check-all-defs!"] [:versioning "repoint-refs!"]]}

    ;; --- Export & distribution: the loader's inverse -------------------
    {:ns graphden.packages.export :defn records->fn-defs
     :say "The loader's INVERSE: typed graph records back to fns.edn-shaped
           fn-def maps. Everything distribution-shaped (publish, install,
           whole-graph download) rides this, and its contract is a fixpoint —
           `parse(export(parse(x))) == parse(x)` — pinned by the
           corpus-fixpoint test over the real core/web/app packages. Read it
           as `process-module` run backwards."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.export :defn ref-kw
     :say "How a ref serializes: BARE keyword when the name is unique
           (canonical minimal form), namespace-QUALIFIED when it's duplicated
           across the export set. Qualification never falls back to bare — a
           version-materialized `@`-ns qualifies as `(keyword \"lib@1-2-0.sub\"
           n)` and the root ns as `(keyword \"\" n)`, both legal in-memory
           values even though no EDN reader could parse their printed form."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.records.wire :defn encode-unreadable-kws
     :say "The EDN-TEXT boundary is the one place those unspellable keywords
           break, so exactly there they become `#graphden/ref \"lib@…/n\"`
           tagged literals before `pr-str`; `wire-readers` decodes them back
           at the package loader and the remote bundle reader. In-memory and
           JSONB paths keep raw keywords — the storage codec round-trips
           them."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.export :defn external-deps
     :say "Scoped export: a namespace SUBTREE becomes a publishable bundle —
           fns under the root are the package's content, and this analysis
           pass turns refs pointing OUT of the subtree into its declared
           `:dependencies` (what install must already have) plus the
           `:package-dependencies` those refs' `@`-versioned namespaces map
           to. The bundle ASSEMBLY around it is graph composition — the
           `:export-namespace` fn-def in registry/registry/fns.edn. This is
           the unit the in-graph registry versions."}
    {:ns graphden.packages.export :defn strip-secret-paths
     :say "The share-safety policy: vault paths are STRIPPED from bundles by
           default (the slot stays a free `[:secret :text]` arg) and the
           `:secrets` manifest lists what was affected — the publisher sees
           the warning, the installer gets a `:needs-definition` todo. A
           bundle is deliberately not a full copy of your secrets."}
    {:file "resources/packages/registry/registry/impls.clj" :defn publish-package-apply
     :say "Publish lands the bundle IN the graph: an immutable, content-hashed
           `:package-version` row (name + semver + fns + declared deps).
           Install is its mirror — `materialize-package-fns` syncs the pinned
           version's fns into a version-qualified `@`-namespace and writes a
           `:package-install` pin; update/rollback rewrites refs between
           versions; fork copies copy-on-write with no pin."}]}

  {:id :boot
   :title "Boot & lifecycle"
   :status :toured
   :summary "How the process starts: the Integrant component graph, the Aero
             config + addon manifest, and the router seams installed at boot."
   :paths ["src/graphden/executor_runtime/" "src/graphden/system/"]
   :after [:storage :packages]
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.executor-runtime.core :defn -main
     :say "**Where the JVM starts.** Four lines that say what a graphden
           process IS: boot the Integrant system, install a shutdown hook,
           optionally open an nREPL (`GRAPHDEN_NREPL_PORT` — the door the
           `clojure` MCP tools come through), then park forever. Nothing
           here serves HTTP; the web server is a `:service` row the
           reconciler starts, several components down."
     :see [[:boot "boot!"] [:services "reconcile-once!"]]}

    {:ns graphden.executor-runtime.core :defn boot!
     :say "Start, or HALT the partial system and exit non-zero. Integrant does
           NOT auto-halt when an init-key throws, and several components have
           already spawned non-daemon threads by then — without this the JVM
           stays half-alive with no shutdown hook and `/health` can pass on a
           boot that actually failed. The partial system rides in the ex-info's
           `:system` key. Read the docstring: it is the incident this function
           exists for."}

    {:ns graphden.system.config :defn read-config
     :say "Configuration is data: Aero reads `system-<profile>.edn`
           (`:dev` / `:test` / `:prod`) with `#ig/ref` and `#var` reader tags
           registered above, then deep-merges any ADDON config fragments named
           in `GRAPHDEN_ADDON_CONFIGS`. That merge is the open-core seam
           (docs/TENANCY_SEAM.md § Addon config manifest) — the private tenancy
           addon REDEFINES core keys and ADDS its own without a single edit to
           this repo. With no addons the merge is a no-op."
     :see [[:platform "platform-tier?"]]}

    {:ns graphden.system.interface :defn start!
     :say "`(ig/init (read-config profile))` — that is the whole system
           lifecycle. The component graph and its dependency edges are
           documented in `graphden.system.core`'s docstring, which exists
           only to `:require` the seven `init.*` namespaces for their
           `defmethod` side effects, so every `ig/init-key` is registered
           before this call. The next four steps walk that graph in
           dependency order."}

    {:ns graphden.system.init.storage :defn ig/init-key :dispatch :db/postgres
     :say "The first component with a socket (`:db/schema` before it is pure —
           it is the `create-builder` + `extend-builder` chain from the
           storage block): open the Postgres pool, run
           `initialize-with-cleanup!` against that schema, seed traits. Note
           `:datasource-wrap` — a `DataSource → DataSource` fn the tenancy
           addon supplies so every connection carries `graphden.current_org`
           for row-level security. Absent in core: a plain pool. `:db/versioned`
           wraps this one in the versioning decorator."
     :see [[:versioning "wrap-with-versioning"]]}

    {:ns graphden.system.init.packages :defn ig/init-key :dispatch :app/packages
     :say "Load the package set — core + web + app, plus the addon's
           `:extra-package-names` (the fns-channel seam) and any external
           Type-2 packages from the operator manifest. Its two siblings just
           below, `:exec/base-fns` and `:exec/fn-entities`, are thin shells
           over `graphden.packages.sync` — this is where **the graph gets its
           content** on every boot."
     :see [[:packages "load-packages"] [:packages "sync-fn-entities-from-packages!"]]}

    {:ns graphden.system.init.exec :defn ig/init-key :dispatch :exec/context
     :say "Assembles the one `ExecutionContext` the whole process runs
           against, and it reads as a list of the seams core exposes:
           auth-provider, request-scope, execute-guard, vault client,
           notify-emitter, fleet forward-hop, and `:compile-storage` — the
           privileged org-agnostic handle `rebuild!` reads the graph
           STRUCTURE through, while data reads stay org-scoped. In
           single-tenant every one of these is the default and the `cond->`
           tail is a no-op."
     :see [[:executor "create-context"]]}

    {:ns graphden.system.init.exec :defn ig/init-key :dispatch :exec/compiled-registry
     :say "Two lines with a ~50s cost: compile every fn in the graph into a
           closure BEFORE the first request. This is the eager half of the
           executor's compile-once story — after this key, `registry` in the
           hot path is a warm holder read, never a cold compile."
     :see [[:executor "rebuild!"] [:executor "registry"]]}

    {:ns graphden.system.route-collection :defn dispatch-first
     :say "The last boot-time seam. Optional subsystems (the `registry`
           package, `/mcp`) and the tenancy addon each install a
           `:router-or-nil` Ring callable here under a distinct key; the core
           handler chain tries them in turn and falls through to the app
           router on `nil`. With nothing installed the collection is `{}`,
           this short-circuits, and single-tenant behaviour is byte-for-byte
           unchanged — the shape every seam in this codebase aims for."}]}

  {:id :web
   :title "Web — routing & the page runtime"
   :status :toured
   :summary "HTTP from socket to response body: routing, middleware as graph
             fns, the hiccup page runtime, and the APIs the editor reads."
   :paths ["resources/packages/web/" "resources/packages/mcp/"
           "src/graphden/crud/types_api.clj"]
   :after [:packages :boot]
   :steps
   [{:file "resources/packages/web/http/impls.clj" :defn http-server
     :say "The http-kit wrapper base-fn: `run-server` spins the listener thread
           and binds the composed Ring handler to a port, recording `:network` /
           `:process` effects. Where a request first enters the web layer, so the
           natural first stop. Note it is a tiny primitive — everything else is
           composed from small base-fns in `web/http/fns.edn`."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/http/impls.clj" :defn realize-request-body
     :say "Fully realizes the incoming request body so downstream graph handlers
           see plain data instead of a one-shot stream — the very next thing that
           happens to a raw request after it lands."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/reitit/impls.clj" :defn ring-handler-fn
     :say "Composes the compiled reitit router with the default handler into the
           single Ring callable http-kit actually invokes per request. The seam
           between the server socket and routing."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/reitit/impls.clj" :defn ring-router-fn
     :say "Builds the bare `reitit.ring/router` from route data — which is itself
           graph-composed vectors and keywords. This is the routing table the
           handler consults to match a path to a handler."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/reitit/impls.clj" :defn ring-create-default-handler-fn
     :say "Constructs the fallback handler reitit uses when nothing matches,
           wrapping the graph-composed 404 / 405 / 406 response maps. The `no
           route` branch."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/reitit/impls.clj" :defn middleware
     :say "The middleware factory: turn a graph `body` (with `:request` and
           `:next-handler` free args) into a reitit `{:name :wrap}` spec folded
           around each route handler. How cross-cutting logic — like the
           branch-routing wrap next — is expressed as a graph fn."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/branch-router/impls.clj" :defn current-branch-router
     :say "Reads the currently-installed per-branch router from a global atom
           (nil before boot installs it). The branch-routing wrap needs this
           router first."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/branch-router/impls.clj" :defn dispatch-to-branch
     :say "The branch-routing wrap: delegate the Ring request to the correct
           per-branch handler resolved from the router above. This is how ONE
           server multiplexes requests across branch-scoped graphs — the package
           side of the versioning block's `dispatch*`."
     :see [[:versioning "dispatch*"]]}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/ring-adapter/impls.clj" :defn authenticate-request
     :say "The auth gate applied to a request inside the handler chain before a
           page or action runs. Sits between routing/dispatch and the handler
           body that will build a response."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/html/impls.clj" :defn hiccup-element
     :say "The generic `[tag attrs children]` constructor behind the graph-native
           page runtime. User-composed pages are fn-defs (`:html-page`,
           components) that emit hiccup through this primitive — including the
           `data-action` attrs that the editor's JavaScript runtime
           (`resources/packages/app/editor/*.js`) binds on click — alongside the `hx-*`
           attrs whose server-rendered `/partials/*` fragments now carry
           logic that once lived in that JS. Where a user's
           page becomes a concrete hiccup tree."
     :see [[:editor "bindActionDispatch"] [:editor "loadPartial"]]}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/html/impls.clj" :defn h-raw
     :say "Wraps a string in a hiccup `RawString` so inline script / style bodies
           (like the editor's bundled JS / CSS) render verbatim instead of being
           HTML-escaped. The escape hatch that lets a composed page embed the JS
           frontend without mangling it."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/html/impls.clj" :defn render-hiccup
     :say "Serializes the assembled hiccup tree into an HTML string, with a
           taint-propagation rule so rendered secrets stay redacted. The point
           where a graph-composed page turns into the response body."}
    {:file "resources/packages/web/http/impls.clj" :defn pick-encoding-fn
     :say "The outbound finalizer is a graph composition now — `:process-response`
           in web/http/fns.edn stringifies headers, applies the `Connection: close`
           policy, and encodes the body, all as fn-defs over single-call
           primitives. This RFC 7231 Accept-Encoding negotiation is the one
           self-contained parse the pipeline keeps in Clojure; the DECISION to
           encode (thresholds, compressible pattern) lives in the graph. The last
           stop as the rendered page leaves the web layer back through http-kit."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.types-api :defn compatible-type-names
     :say "The server-side type-picker sweep behind
           `/partials/compatible-type-options`. It filters the
           `type-name-kinds` roster to the names that can legally narrow
           `expected` in ONE alias-aware `subtype?` pass — replacing the
           editor's former per-name fan-out of ~50 parallel
           `POST /api/types/compatible` requests per picker open. The
           graph-first migration's clearest case: a client loop collapses
           into one server render."}
    {:ns graphden.crud.types-api :defn api-rich-types
     :say "The `/api/types` wire-shaping layer the editor loads at boot and
           after every mutation: the augmented rich-type snapshot with
           `bulk-omitted-fields` stripped from each entry. The old
           `?fn=<name>` per-fn full-entry backfill is gone with its only
           client consumer — the return-type-rule popover is a server
           partial now, and the layout strip facts read the raw registry
           directly. How the type-checker's registry reaches the browser as
           a lean payload."}
    {:file "resources/packages/mcp/mcp/impls.clj" :defn sync-fn-defs-branch!
     :say "The web layer has a second client: an AI. The optional `mcp`
           package serves `/mcp` (JSON-RPC) through the same per-branch
           router, and this is its only write primitive — sync fn-defs into a
           branch, then delta-invalidate THAT branch's registry, not the
           request's own (the AI writes to `ai/…` while its POST rides main).
           The key design point is in the docstring: writes go through the
           very `sync-fns-to-storage!` the package loader uses, so a
           proposal from an AI meets the same cycle / collision / type
           constraints as a human's `fns.edn`."
     :see [[:executor "sync-fns-to-storage!"] [:boot "dispatch-first"]]}]}

  {:id :layout
   :title "Layout — the editor's graph view"
   :status :toured
   :summary "Turn raw fn/slot/binding rows into the placed cards, edges and
             server-computed facts the editor renders."
   :paths ["src/graphden/layout/"]
   :after [:web]
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.layout.data :defn load-graph-entities-uncached
     :say "Stage 1's data pull — every fn / slot / fn-slot / binding /
           list-item row in one branch-cached batch (versioned) or five
           vanilla queries. The whole pipeline is a pure transform over this
           snapshot; a per-context graph cache keeps hover-frequency layout
           requests from re-reading storage."}
    {:ns graphden.layout.data :defn derive-fn-slot-views
     :say "Synthesizes the per-fn 'arg view' rows the walkers consume: for
           each fn, its inherited slot surface with bindings overlaid
           closer-fn-wins, plus the `:source-id` chains that let an edge
           attribute an inherited slot to the ancestor that declared it.
           The slot/binding model's read-side counterpart."}
    {:ns graphden.layout.graph :defn build-graph-elements
     :say "Stage 2's orchestrator — dispatches the cycle-guarded `process-*`
           walkers over the expansion spec, then pipes the accumulated state
           through the post-processing transforms (optional/HOF badges,
           captured-edge migration, overlay dedup, strip facts). Produces
           the node/edge element lists the client draws verbatim."}
    {:ns graphden.layout.core :defn compute-layout-matrix
     :say "Stages 3+ — grid placement: roots at column zero, dependents
           right of their sources, rows packed to avoid overlap, plus the
           validation summary. Pixel geometry stays client-side (measured
           reflow off the real DOM); the server owns the grid topology."}
    {:ns graphden.layout.strip-facts :defn annotate
     :say "The editor-facing tail: attach server-computed FACTS to each
           fn-node — the inherited return-type alias, the rule-owning
           base-fn (`registry/rule-owner-of`), and the transitive
           branch-local seed (`branch-local/branch-local-seed`). These
           replaced three hand-written client mirrors of the same walks;
           the client renders facts, it doesn't re-derive them."
     :see [[:versioning "effective-branch-local?"]]}]}

  {:id :editor
   :title "Editor frontend — the browser half"
   :status :toured
   :summary "The ~25k lines of plain ES that draw the graph, bind the edits
             and mount the server's partials. No build step, no framework."
   :paths ["resources/packages/app/editor/" "resources/packages/web/runtime/"]
   :after [:web :layout]
   :steps
   [{:file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-main.js" :defn initGraph
     :say "**Start here for anything you can click.** The editor is ~75 plain
           `.js` files concatenated into one bundle in a fixed order (no
           imports, no build step — the per-module map is in
           [docs/EDITOR_MODULES.md](../EDITOR_MODULES.md)), and this is the
           boot. Note the shape of the fetches: the namespace TREE goes out
           alone first, because its status answers the auth question for
           everyone — only a non-401 fires the three secondary loads. Fn
           leaves and per-fn slots/bindings arrive lazily; a 401 sends the
           user to sign in rather than into a red fatal banner."
     :see [[:editor "authFetch"]]}

    {:file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-data.js" :defn buildLookups
     :say "The client mirror of the compile layer's `build-lookups`: the same
           raw fn / slot / fn-slot / binding / list-item rows, indexed into
           the O(1) Maps every render path reads (`fnMap`, `slotByFnAndName`,
           `bindingByFnSlot`, `itemsByBinding`, the namespace-path map). Every
           comment here is a fixed O(N×M) scan — this function runs on every
           mutation, so it is where client-side graph work gets slow."
     :see [[:executor "build-lookups"]]}

    {:file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-graph-model.js" :defn graph
     :say "The graph itself: two Maps plus two adjacency indexes and an
           `animating` flag. This is *all* that remained of cytoscape when it
           was removed — the library had been reduced to a node/edge store and
           a position tween, so it became this file. `window.graphView` is the
           surface Playwright tests wait on (`ready()`, `animating()`), which
           is why the browser-test suite never needed rewriting."}

    {:file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-layout.js" :defn fetchBackendLayout
     :say "The client does NOT lay the graph out. It POSTs the selected root
           plus the per-node expansion spec to `/api/graph/layout` and gets
           back nodes, edges and a `nodeId → {x, y, row, col}` map. This is
           the wire crossing into the previous block: the server owns grid
           topology, the client owns pixels."
     :see [[:layout "build-graph-elements"] [:layout "compute-layout-matrix"]]}

    {:file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-render.js" :defn renderGraph
     :say "One render cycle, and the reason it is not a one-liner: prime the
           selected fn's subtree, capture an ANCHOR node before the await so
           the canvas doesn't jump under the cursor, fetch the layout, diff it
           against the live graph, then re-measure — card heights are only
           known after the overlays exist, so the server's grid is corrected
           against measured DOM and the nodes settle onto the result."}

    {:file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-overlay-fn.js" :defn createFnOverlay
     :say "A node is not drawn — it is an absolutely-positioned HTML card
           riding the layer transform (edges are SVG; everything else is
           DOM). This builds one fn card: the inheritance levels from
           `parent-ids`, the use-site header when the card is an expanded
           child rather than the navigation root, and a row per slot. The
           slot/binding model of the very first block, made clickable."
     :see [[:layout "derive-fn-slot-views"]]}

    {:file "resources/packages/web/runtime/graphden-runtime.js" :defn bindActionDispatch
     :say "The pivot from client logic to server-rendered HTML. ONE delegated
           listener per host resolves `data-action=\"…\"` against a registry
           filled by `registerActionHandler`, so a fragment the server just
           rendered becomes interactive with no per-fragment wiring. This file
           is platform-shared: it is bundled into the editor AND served
           standalone at `/assets/graphden-runtime.js`, so user-composed pages
           get the same dispatch. Read the bind-once comment — without it a
           reused singleton popover fires `delete-fn` N times on the Nth open."
     :see [[:web "hiccup-element"]]}

    {:file "resources/packages/web/runtime/graphden-runtime.js" :defn loadPartial
     :say "Fetch an HTML partial, swap it into a host, re-bind the dispatcher.
           That is the whole `/partials/*` client contract
           ([docs/PARTIALS.md](../PARTIALS.md)) — the migration path by which
           popovers that used to be hand-built DOM became graph-native
           fn-defs. The supersession token is the subtle part: two concurrent
           loads into one host must let the LATEST call win, not the fastest
           response."}

    {:file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-row-actions.js" :defn loadRowActionsContent
     :say "The migration in the concrete: the `⋯` row menu is a server partial
           in four contexts, and the client only builds the query string,
           caches the returned HTML per URL, and recomputes the one state that
           MUST be fresh (add-MI compatibility, against current `lookups`) —
           see [docs/EDITOR_ROW_ACTIONS.md](../EDITOR_ROW_ACTIONS.md) for why
           this popover moved and the others stayed JS."}

    {:file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-edit-modes.js" :defn enterArgValueEditMode
     :say "How an edit actually happens. The control is not chosen here: the
           client POSTs the slot to `/api/value-form` and the graph's
           value-form registry answers with hiccup naming a widget — the
           editor routes on `data-form-widget` and knows no type tags at all.
           Note `onSaved`: a value change alters binding rows, so `lookups` is
           stale and the handler re-fetches rather than re-rendering."
     :see [[:crud "render-repr"]]}

    {:file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-auth.js" :defn authFetch
     :say "Every mutating call in the editor goes through this. It is 12 lines
           and worth all of them: a 401 clears the stored credential and
           re-opens the sign-in popover in place. The trap to internalize
           before you write editor code — `authFetch` and `authMutate`
           **resolve** on 4xx, they do not throw. A caller that only wraps
           them in `try/catch` counts a rejected write as a success."}

    {:file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-tour.js" :defn _tourTick
     :say "Closing the loop back to the user tutorial: the in-editor guided
           lessons are graph content (`app.tour/_tour-lessons`, served at
           `GET /api/tour`) and this file is only the engine that interprets
           them. Each tick clears the reader-state traps that make a lesson
           unfinishable — a collapsed Explorer, a kind lens hiding the very fn
           the step names, a target scrolled out of view — then polls the
           step's declarative check and advances. A tour that can dead-end is
           worse than no tour, which is what every guard here is paying for."}]}

  {:id :services
   :title "Services & the fleet"
   :status :toured
   :summary "Desired-state service registry, the reconciler/supervisor, and the
             dynamic multi-executor fleet controller."
   :paths ["src/graphden/services/" "src/graphden/fleet/"]
   :after [:executor]
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.schema.services.schema :defn extend-builder
     :say "Start with what a service ROW is: this registers the `:service`
           entity plus the `:restart-policy` / `:cardinality` enums. A service is
           a desired-state `keep this fn-id running` declaration — not versioned,
           no args table — the thing everything downstream reconciles toward."}
    {:ns graphden.schema.services.schema :defn effective-pool-size
     :say "Resolves a row's cardinality into an advisory-lock slot count:
           `:per-pod` → no lock, `:singleton` → 1, `:pool` → N. You need this
           singleton-vs-per-pod-vs-pool model before the reconciler's lock gating
           makes sense."}
    {:ns graphden.services.reconciler :defn diff-desired
     :say "The pure policy core: given the enabled DB rows (desired) and the
           in-process `running` set (actual), return `{:to-start :to-stop}`. It
           isolates the reconcile DECISION from side effects — the cleanest first
           look at the loop."}
    {:ns graphden.services.reconciler :defn reconcile-once!
     :say "One reconcile pass: read enabled rows, apply `diff-desired`, detect
           config drift, and race advisory-lock slots. This is BOTH the
           edge-triggered handler (fired by `:service` CRUD / NOTIFY) and the
           level-triggered periodic tick — the placeholder it recomputes each
           pass is exactly what heals a crashed lock-owner on the next tick."}
    {:ns graphden.services.reconciler :defn start-service!
     :say "The supervisor that actually keeps a fn running: invoke the target fn
           via the executor and, on a startup failure, sleep + retry up to N
           times per the row's `:restart-policy`, stamping `:start-failed-at` when
           exhausted so reconcile will not busy-loop. The `how a service stays up`
           answer that closes out the services half."}
    {:ns graphden.fleet.placement :defn placement-for
     :say "Opens the fleet half with its shared source of truth: the
           `:placement` row mapping `(org, entry-fn-id)` → the executor holding
           that cell, highest-epoch wins. Every fleet component reads or writes
           this map, so it is the right foundation before routing and packing."}
    {:ns graphden.fleet.router :defn forward-or-nil
     :say "The internal forward-hop: when a request lands on a pod that does not
           hold the target cell, look up the holder in the placement map and
           HTTP-forward there instead of returning `421`. How the placement map
           becomes live cross-fleet routing."}
    {:ns graphden.fleet.metrics :defn cell-weight
     :say "Folds a cell's structural cost (forward-closure fn-count) and live
           load (the org's in-flight executions) into the single scalar the
           packer and rebalancer order by. The load signal that drives every
           placement decision below."}
    {:ns graphden.fleet.packer :defn pack
     :say "The LPT (longest-processing-time-first) greedy packer: sort cells
           heaviest-first and drop each on the least-loaded executor — the classic
           makespan 2-approximation. It answers `what SHOULD the placement be` for
           a from-scratch layout."}
    {:ns graphden.fleet.rebalance :defn rebalance
     :say "The churn-minimising planner: rather than diff against a fresh pack,
           do steepest-descent (move one cell busiest → lightest while it strictly
           reduces spread) with a `:min-improvement` hysteresis floor, emitting
           the FEWEST moves. How a running fleet is nudged toward balance without
           thrashing."}
    {:ns graphden.fleet.control-loop :defn plan-tick
     :say "The pure brain of one control pass: always emit initial placements for
           unheld cells, but gate rebalance moves behind `:sustain-ticks` so
           imbalance must PERSIST (not just spike) before cells move. Composes the
           packer + rebalancer into the actual control policy."}
    {:ns graphden.fleet.control-loop :defn run-tick!
     :say "The side-effecting driver the leader-locked component runs each tick:
           read live cells + current placement, call `plan-tick`, then realise
           each initial placement and sustained move through the `move-fn` seam.
           The loop that ties the whole fleet together."}
    {:ns graphden.fleet.controller :defn move-cell!
     :say "Executes a single relocation as `run-tick!`'s `move-fn`: the load →
           flip-epoch → evict dance that keeps the cell continuously served
           (target compiles first, placement epoch bumps to reroute, source evicts
           last). How an abstract `move` decision safely becomes a live cell
           migration across pods."}]}

  {:id :platform
   :title "Platform seams & hardening"
   :status :toured
   :summary "The seams the multi-tenant addon plugs into (docs/TENANCY_SEAM.md)
             and the hardening layers the core itself carries — effect gate,
             SSRF egress guard, alerting, BYO executor."
   :paths ["src/graphden/tenancy/" "src/graphden/auth/" "src/graphden/clients/"
           "src/graphden/monitoring/" "src/graphden/byo.clj"
           "src/graphden/system/sse.clj"]
   :after [:services]
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.tenancy.context :defn platform-tier?
     :say "The one tenancy namespace that lives in core: `*current-org*` is the
           per-request thread-local the private addon binds, and this predicate
           is how core code asks `am I the platform, or a tenant?` without
           depending on the policy. Everything org-scoped downstream keys off
           this seam."}
    {:ns graphden.auth.provider :defn single-token-provider
     :say "Auth is provider-driven: core ships this single-token default behind
           the `:auth/provider` Integrant key, and the addon swaps in its
           session/user provider. Note the constant-time compare — the default
           is small, but it is still a real credential check."}
    {:ns graphden.executor.compile-runtime :defn cloud-forbidden-effects
     :say "The effect gate's registry of what a sandboxed graph must NOT do —
           env / io / network / process / raw-sql — with the docstring
           explaining why `:raw-sql` exists separately from `:db`. The gate
           mechanism (`record-effect!` throwing outside `*allowed-effects*`)
           lives in core; the addon only chooses the policy sets."}
    {:ns graphden.clients.egress :defn check-target!
     :say "The SSRF egress guard a tenant's outbound call passes before dialing:
           fail-closed resolution of the hostname, rejecting loopback / RFC1918 /
           link-local / cloud-metadata targets. Pair it with `validating-dns`
           below in the same file — the connect-time re-check that closes the
           DNS-rebind TOCTOU."}
    {:ns graphden.web.hiccup-sanitize :defn sanitize-hiccup
     :say "An allowlist walk over an EDN hiccup tree — permitted tags,
           permitted attrs, a depth cap and a node budget, returning nil for
           'no representable output'. This is the gate on every path where
           GRAPH-authored markup reaches the editor's own DOM: typed value
           representations, component previews, server partials rendering
           user content. Anything not on the list collapses to escaped text
           rather than being rejected loudly — the sanitizer's job is to make
           the surface safe, not to argue with the author."
     :see [[:crud "render-repr"]]}
    {:ns graphden.util.counters :defn count!
     :say "Observability starts smaller than you expect: a process-global,
           monotonic `{event → long}` atom. Everything in docs/MONITORING.md —
           the usage rollups, the Prometheus scrape, the alerter's window
           below — is a diff of two `snapshot`s of this map. Monotonic on
           purpose: readers diff rather than reset, so two of them can never
           invalidate each other's window."}
    {:ns graphden.monitoring.alerts :defn decide
     :say "The built-in alerter's pure policy: given the last window's counters
           and the previous state, decide fire / resolve / stay quiet. All the
           side effects (scheduler, webhook POST) live elsewhere — this is the
           testable core of docs/MONITORING.md's second alerting path."}
    {:ns graphden.system.sse :defn start-relay!
     :say "The SSE invalidation relay: the hosted side fans branch/org
           invalidation events out to subscribed external executors so a BYO
           executor's compiled registry stays fresh without polling. The
           freshness half of the BYO story."}
    {:ns graphden.storage.remote.core :defn RemoteStorage
     :say "The read side of BYO: a `StorageCRUD` leaf whose rows are an ATOM
           of `{entity → [row …]}` fetched from the hub over HTTP, with every
           write arm throwing `read-only!`. The customer's executor compiles
           and runs the same graph while the hub stays the only writer —
           and `refresh!` swaps in a fresh bundle without rebuilding the
           record, which is what the SSE relay below triggers."
     :see [[:storage "StorageCRUD"]]}
    {:ns graphden.byo :defn start-byo!
     :say "The customer-hosted executor assembly: RemoteStorage over HTTP as the
           read-only graph source, the SSE source for invalidation, and a direct
           http-server — the same core, assembled for someone else's hardware.
           Closes the platform block: the seams above are exactly what make this
           assembly possible."}]}

  {:id :accounts
   :title "Accounts — the open identity module"
   :status :toured
   :summary "The opt-in identity layer (docs/ACCOUNTS.md): one account, many
             sign-in identities (password / Google / GitHub / Telegram),
             hashed sessions, email verification, TOTP 2FA, and the /auth/*
             + /login surface — all OFF unless the addon fragment is enabled."
   :paths ["src/graphden/accounts/"]
   :steps
   [{:ns graphden.accounts.core :defn resolve-social-identity!
     :say "The heart of account-LINKING: a social sign-in resolves to an
           existing identity, auto-links onto an account that owns the same
           VERIFIED email, or creates a fresh account — in that order. One
           person, many sign-in methods, one stable account-id for authz."}
    {:ns graphden.accounts.core :defn mint-session!
     :say "Sessions are rows, not JWTs: the raw token exists exactly once (the
           return value); storage keeps only its SHA-256. `kind` overloads the
           same row for verify links and pending-2FA steps — kinds outside
           nil/\"api\" never authenticate, so a verify link can't become a
           bearer."}
    {:ns graphden.accounts.provider :defn accounts-provider
     :say "The module's AuthProvider: bearer OR the HttpOnly gd_session cookie
           (an OAuth redirect can't carry a bearer) → an org-AGNOSTIC principal.
           Org is deliberately absent — the tenancy layer resolves it from
           membership, which is what keeps this module open-core."}
    {:ns graphden.accounts.routes :defn make-router
     :say "The whole HTTP surface as one route-collection Ring router:
           password + social + telegram callbacks, verify links, identities
           list/unlink, TOTP enroll/confirm/login-step, and the /login +
           /account pages. Installed before the graph router; returns nil to
           fall through, so an instance without the addon never sees it. The
           page/email PRESENTATION renders through the injected graph seam
           (`app.auth-pages` on the platform ctx) with the built-in Clojure
           shell as the graph-outage fallback — see `graph-page` just above."}
    {:ns graphden.accounts.totp :defn valid?
     :say "RFC 6238 in ~100 lines: base32, HOTP, a ±1-step skew window and a
           constant-time compare, verified against the RFC's reference vectors.
           The enrollment/confirm flow that uses it lives in accounts.core."}]}
]}
